1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,831
This is LabeloxAV, a data engine for autonomous driving built
around Indian roads.

2
00:00:05,831 --> 00:00:15,622
Everything you are about to see runs against a live database with 381 driving sessions, 42,766 frames and 598,179 labelled
objects in it.

3
00:00:15,622 --> 00:00:18,142
No screen in this tour is a mock up.

4
00:00:18,142 --> 00:00:26,133
Where a number is small or a page is empty, that is the real
state of the system and the narration will say so.

5
00:00:26,133 --> 00:00:29,265
The product is ten platforms over a single data spine.

6
00:00:29,265 --> 00:00:39,607
Annotation, review, measurement, governance, edge deployment and the rest each get their own surface, but they all read and
write the same sessions, frames, objects and tracks.

7
00:00:39,607 --> 00:00:44,866
That is why a correction made in the editor changes what the
gate measures an hour later.

8
00:00:44,866 --> 00:00:48,968
Work is scoped by project, and each project belongs to a
domain pack.

9
00:00:48,968 --> 00:00:56,389
The autonomous driving pack brings the road ontology, the camera and LiDAR sensor model and the traffic scenario
vocabulary.

10
00:00:56,389 --> 00:01:01,999
A second pack for physical security shares the same spine and
none of the road specific code.

11
00:01:01,999 --> 00:01:03,012
Data arrives here.

12
00:01:03,012 --> 00:01:11,886
The importer takes dashcam video, ROS bags, MCAP recordings and raw sensor drives, and turns each one into a session with
frames at a fixed timestamp.

13
00:01:11,886 --> 00:01:14,626
For this tour I ingested three real recordings.

14
00:01:14,626 --> 00:01:22,666
Two are dashcam clips from the owner of this machine, and one is a KITTI drive from Karlsruhe with a sixty four beam LiDAR
on the roof.

15
00:01:22,666 --> 00:01:25,457
Most teams already have labels somewhere else.

16
00:01:25,457 --> 00:01:35,939
The migration path reads COCO, YOLO, Pascal and CVAT exports, maps their class names onto this ontology, and records the
mapping so that nothing silently changes meaning.

17
00:01:35,939 --> 00:01:39,599
Classes it cannot map are held back rather than guessed at.

18
00:01:39,599 --> 00:01:42,080
Every ingested session lands in the inspector.

19
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:49,136
This is a Foxglove class viewer built into the product, so you can check a recording before you spend any labelling effort on
it.

20
00:01:49,136 --> 00:01:51,617
The health gate at the top is the useful part.

21
00:01:51,617 --> 00:01:59,666
It refuses a session with broken timestamps, missing calibration or a camera that dropped out, instead of letting
the problem reach the labellers.

22
00:01:59,666 --> 00:02:01,130
This is the KITTI drive.

23
00:02:01,130 --> 00:02:06,668
The timeline shows every topic on one clock, the camera
alongside the LiDAR and the GPS.

24
00:02:06,668 --> 00:02:15,133
The panels are native, not an embedded iframe, so a click on the timeline moves the point cloud, the image and the ego pose
together.

25
00:02:15,133 --> 00:02:18,302
Calibration is what makes three dimensional work possible.

26
00:02:18,302 --> 00:02:27,307
This session carries real intrinsics from the KITTI rig, a focal length of seven hundred and twenty one pixels over a
twelve forty two by three seventy five image.

27
00:02:27,307 --> 00:02:32,533
Without this the system can still draw boxes, but it cannot
tell you how far away anything is.

28
00:02:32,533 --> 00:02:34,369
Here is the vehicle's own motion.

29
00:02:34,369 --> 00:02:36,837
Speed, yaw rate and the trajectory it drove.

30
00:02:36,837 --> 00:02:41,313
The KITTI import brought 154 measured ego poses from the GPS
and inertial unit.

31
00:02:41,313 --> 00:02:52,733
Before this import the entire corpus had three satellite fixes across forty one thousand frames, which is the honest reason most of the three dimensional work here was estimated rather
than measured.

32
00:02:52,733 --> 00:02:56,311
A dataset is a frozen selection of frames with a content hash.

33
00:02:56,311 --> 00:03:06,400
Two people training on the same dataset identifier are training on exactly the same images and labels, which is the
only way a comparison between two models means anything.

34
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:08,133
Labelling starts automatically.

35
00:03:08,133 --> 00:03:14,486
You choose a session and the classes you care about, and the
auto label runner works through it batch by batch.

36
00:03:14,486 --> 00:03:18,586
Batch by batch is a deliberate constraint, not an
implementation detail.

37
00:03:18,586 --> 00:03:28,000
The runner holds a GPU slot, checks free video memory between batches, and stops if a training job wants the card, so a
labelling run cannot take the machine down.

38
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:32,889
This is the annotation canvas with real machine predictions on
a real frame.

39
00:03:32,889 --> 00:03:34,975
The tools run down the left side.

40
00:03:34,975 --> 00:03:40,646
Box, polygon, segment anything, lane, drivable area,
keypoints, amodal extent and depth.

41
00:03:40,646 --> 00:03:48,533
Every tool is registered in one place, so the keyboard shortcut you see in the panel is the shortcut that actually
fires.

42
00:03:48,533 --> 00:03:51,191
The describe tool is the one worth stopping on.

43
00:03:51,191 --> 00:04:00,033
You type what you want in plain words, for example a cow standing on the median, and an open vocabulary detector
proposes it with a segment anything mask.

44
00:04:00,033 --> 00:04:08,933
This exists because the ontology will never have a word for everything on an Indian road, and the alternative is that
those objects go unlabelled forever.

45
00:04:08,933 --> 00:04:12,847
Lanes and drivable surface are annotated as geometry rather
than boxes.

46
00:04:12,847 --> 00:04:17,489
The drivable mask now covers ninety nine point nine percent of
frames in the corpus.

47
00:04:17,489 --> 00:04:21,515
It used to cover under four percent, and the gap was not a
model problem.

48
00:04:21,515 --> 00:04:25,933
Three separate consumers were reading the masks in a format
nobody had written.

49
00:04:25,933 --> 00:04:30,209
Attributes are swept across many objects at once instead of
one at a time.

50
00:04:30,209 --> 00:04:36,242
Occlusion level, truncation, whether a rider is wearing a
helmet, whether a vehicle is parked or moving.

51
00:04:36,242 --> 00:04:44,266
One pass over a hundred crops of the same class is far faster than a hundred visits to the editor, and it keeps the
judgement consistent.

52
00:04:44,266 --> 00:04:46,590
Road furniture gets its own surface.

53
00:04:46,590 --> 00:04:49,511
Signs, signals, poles, barriers and markings.

54
00:04:49,511 --> 00:05:00,066
Signs carry an IRC sixty seven code, which is the Indian standard, so a mandatory sign and a cautionary sign are
distinguishable without reading the pictogram.

55
00:05:00,066 --> 00:05:02,366
Behind the auto labelling sits an ensemble.

56
00:05:02,366 --> 00:05:06,417
Several detectors run over the same frame and their outputs
are reconciled.

57
00:05:06,417 --> 00:05:09,374
Where they agree the label is cheap and probably right.

58
00:05:09,374 --> 00:05:15,999
Where they disagree is exactly where a human should be looking, and that disagreement is mined rather than averaged
away.

59
00:05:15,999 --> 00:05:19,641
Agreement between models becomes pseudo ground truth for
training.

60
00:05:19,641 --> 00:05:22,611
One lesson from building this is recorded on the page.

61
00:05:22,611 --> 00:05:27,767
Choosing the teacher pair by how many predictions each model
made gave nine consensus labels.

62
00:05:27,767 --> 00:05:32,866
Choosing by how many frames the two models shared gave one
thousand one hundred and ninety.

63
00:05:32,866 --> 00:05:36,551
Machine labels are proposals until a person rules on them.

64
00:05:36,551 --> 00:05:46,182
The queue orders work by how much a verdict is worth, combining model uncertainty, class rarity and whether a gate
is currently blocked on that class.

65
00:05:46,182 --> 00:05:54,133
There are 528,793 objects waiting in review right now, which
tells you honestly how far ahead of the humans the machine is.

66
00:05:54,133 --> 00:05:56,999
Grid review shows many crops of one class at once.

67
00:05:56,999 --> 00:06:05,598
The eye is very good at spotting the odd one out in a wall of similar images, and very bad at staying calibrated across a
thousand single decisions.

68
00:06:05,598 --> 00:06:09,400
Accept or reject applies to everything selected in one
keystroke.

69
00:06:09,400 --> 00:06:12,856
Rapid review is the keyboard only mode for a long session.

70
00:06:12,856 --> 00:06:14,554
One crop, one key, next crop.

71
00:06:14,554 --> 00:06:22,800
It records how long each verdict took, because the cost of a label in minutes is the number the whole economics of this
system rests on.

72
00:06:22,800 --> 00:06:28,547
This is the full annotation table, filterable by class, state,
source, session and reviewer.

73
00:06:28,547 --> 00:06:30,379
State is the important column.

74
00:06:30,379 --> 00:06:36,505
A label is in review, auto accepted, settled or accepted, and
accepted means a person ruled on it.

75
00:06:36,505 --> 00:06:38,779
The machine can never write accepted.

76
00:06:38,779 --> 00:06:44,400
That is enforced in code and there is a test that fails the
build if any code path tries.

77
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:47,351
Any single label opens to its full history.

78
00:06:47,351 --> 00:06:57,470
Which model proposed it, at what confidence, which human touched it and when, what it was before each change, and which
training run consumed it.

79
00:06:57,470 --> 00:07:02,600
You can answer where did this label come from for any of
598,179 objects.

80
00:07:02,600 --> 00:07:04,556
Quality is measured, not asserted.

81
00:07:04,556 --> 00:07:14,279
Precision comes from stratified samples with Wilson confidence intervals, so a class with twelve judged crops reports a wide
interval rather than a confident number.

82
00:07:14,279 --> 00:07:16,414
Unmeasured is reported as unmeasured.

83
00:07:16,414 --> 00:07:19,200
It is never reported as zero and never as fine.

84
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:21,344
Errors are typed rather than counted.

85
00:07:21,344 --> 00:07:33,133
A missed detection, a duplicate, a class confusion and a loose box are four different problems with four different fixes, and lumping them into one accuracy number hides which one you
actually have.

86
00:07:33,133 --> 00:07:34,898
This page measures the reviewers.

87
00:07:34,898 --> 00:07:42,732
Control samples with a known answer are mixed into the queue, so agreement between people can be computed without anybody
re-reviewing anybody.

88
00:07:42,732 --> 00:07:44,553
A finding worth admitting is here.

89
00:07:44,553 --> 00:07:53,600
Thirty thousand eight hundred and sixty three of thirty thousand eight hundred and sixty five reviews carry no timing,
because the field was only wired up recently.

90
00:07:53,600 --> 00:07:58,707
An object across frames is a track, and a track is the unit
that matters for driving.

91
00:07:58,707 --> 00:08:00,591
The corpus holds 13,807 of them.

92
00:08:00,591 --> 00:08:05,273
Eighty four percent of tracks used to blink out and restart as
a new identity.

93
00:08:05,273 --> 00:08:12,933
Interpolation across gaps had been written but never run, and turning it on filled one hundred and thirty seven thousand
gaps.

94
00:08:12,933 --> 00:08:15,391
Corrections apply to the track, not the frame.

95
00:08:15,391 --> 00:08:23,419
Relabelling a motorcycle as a rider used to reach one frame out of ninety three, because the editor and the review queue
took different write paths.

96
00:08:23,419 --> 00:08:27,133
They now share one, and the propagation is visible on this
timeline.

97
00:08:27,133 --> 00:08:31,057
A vehicle with several cameras sees the same object more than
once.

98
00:08:31,057 --> 00:08:37,299
With calibration, a box drawn in one view projects into every
other view in the rig, so you annotate once.

99
00:08:37,299 --> 00:08:43,600
The corpus has six multi camera sessions, so the measured
benefit here is small and I will not inflate it.

100
00:08:43,600 --> 00:08:47,093
Between two labelled frames the vehicle moved a known
distance.

101
00:08:47,093 --> 00:08:56,333
That motion alone predicts where a static object should appear next, without running any model, and it is enough to carry a
label forward through a stretch of road.

102
00:08:56,333 --> 00:09:04,095
When a track breaks behind a bus and reappears, the recovery pass tries to rejoin the two halves using appearance and
predicted position.

103
00:09:04,095 --> 00:09:11,000
A rejoined track is marked as rejoined rather than silently
merged, because a wrong merge is worse than two short tracks.

104
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,390
This is where the tour gets its real LiDAR.

105
00:09:13,390 --> 00:09:18,909
The KITTI drive brought 154 point clouds from a sixty four
beam Velodyne, about 18 million points.

106
00:09:18,909 --> 00:09:28,467
Before this import the corpus had four hundred and fifty three thousand points in total, all of them estimated from single
camera depth rather than measured by a laser.

107
00:09:28,467 --> 00:09:32,412
Cuboids are drawn in the point cloud with the camera image
beside them.

108
00:09:32,412 --> 00:09:39,400
The ego trajectory runs underneath, so a box can be placed in
the world frame and stays put while the vehicle moves past it.

109
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:42,350
The linked view keeps the image and the cloud in step.

110
00:09:42,350 --> 00:09:48,028
Click a two dimensional box and the matching three dimensional
box highlights, and the other way round.

111
00:09:48,028 --> 00:09:50,533
This is also how the lifted boxes are checked.

112
00:09:50,533 --> 00:09:56,600
A cuboid estimated from camera depth alone is compared against
the laser points that actually fall inside it.

113
00:09:56,600 --> 00:09:58,844
Sessions on a map, by route and by city.

114
00:09:58,844 --> 00:10:06,267
The locations here are aggregated into cells rather than drawn as raw points, and I will come back to why in the privacy
chapter.

115
00:10:06,267 --> 00:10:08,266
Verdyx is where models are measured.

116
00:10:08,266 --> 00:10:15,062
Accuracy is reported per class with an interval, and against a
frozen gold set that no training run is allowed to touch.

117
00:10:15,062 --> 00:10:18,603
There are 15 registered models, each with its lineage
recorded.

118
00:10:18,603 --> 00:10:23,400
What it was trained from, which teacher it distilled, and
which dataset hash it saw.

119
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:26,068
An average number hides the cases that matter.

120
00:10:26,068 --> 00:10:30,931
Performance is sliced by night, rain, occlusion and by the
safety critical classes.

121
00:10:30,931 --> 00:10:33,065
Frames are also perturbed on purpose.

122
00:10:33,065 --> 00:10:38,758
Occlusion, dusk, rain, fog and motion blur are applied to real
images and the model is re-scored.

123
00:10:38,758 --> 00:10:48,067
The current champion loses twenty four points of motorcycle recall under occlusion, which is the kind of thing an average
accuracy score will never tell you.

124
00:10:48,067 --> 00:10:51,792
A scorecard is the summary a person actually reads before a
release.

125
00:10:51,792 --> 00:10:57,742
Per class recall and precision, the slices that regressed, the
gate result and the reason if it was blocked.

126
00:10:57,742 --> 00:11:02,134
It is generated from the measurement run, so it cannot drift
from the evidence.

127
00:11:02,134 --> 00:11:07,630
Training runs are scheduled here and they are the only thing
allowed to hold the GPU for a long time.

128
00:11:07,630 --> 00:11:14,005
Everything else in the system checks whether training holds
the card before it asks for it, and backs off if it does.

129
00:11:14,005 --> 00:11:21,534
That single rule is why labelling, embedding, depth and three dimensional lifting can all run on one desktop without
fighting each other.

130
00:11:21,534 --> 00:11:25,612
Lineage connects a deployed model back to every label it
learned from.

131
00:11:25,612 --> 00:11:32,467
If a label is found to be wrong, this answers which models saw
it, and therefore which measurements are now suspect.

132
00:11:32,467 --> 00:11:34,646
Events are the moments worth finding.

133
00:11:34,646 --> 00:11:39,308
Hard braking, a cut in, a near miss, a pedestrian crossing
against the signal.

134
00:11:39,308 --> 00:11:43,061
They are detected from ego dynamics and object tracks
together.

135
00:11:43,061 --> 00:11:45,180
An honest caveat from building this.

136
00:11:45,180 --> 00:11:54,200
The dynamics signal in this corpus is noisier than the events it is being asked to find, so the detector is tuned to over
report and let a human cut.

137
00:11:54,200 --> 00:12:03,467
Events are searchable by description, so you can ask for every time a rider overtook on the left while the vehicle was
braking, and get frames rather than a report.

138
00:12:03,467 --> 00:12:06,138
This is the part I would show a sceptic first.

139
00:12:06,138 --> 00:12:09,818
The system proposes its own work, but it cannot approve
itself.

140
00:12:09,818 --> 00:12:17,534
Every autonomous action is a proposal with evidence, a named rule that fired, and a revert button that actually undoes the
writes.

141
00:12:17,534 --> 00:12:21,508
A class is signed off by sequential testing rather than a
fixed sample.

142
00:12:21,508 --> 00:12:26,163
A clean class proves itself in far fewer verdicts and a bad
one fails in a handful.

143
00:12:26,163 --> 00:12:28,093
Accept is deliberately conjunctive.

144
00:12:28,093 --> 00:12:39,334
The sequential test and the confidence interval must both agree before anything is accepted, while a rejection is allowed to fire early, because rejecting early is the safe
direction to be wrong in.

145
00:12:39,334 --> 00:12:44,740
The agent layer plans labelling work, criticises its own
output and reconciles conflicts.

146
00:12:44,740 --> 00:12:49,900
It works in chunks, and every chunk is one recorded run with
the exact rows it wrote.

147
00:12:49,900 --> 00:12:53,401
Reverting a run is a real database operation, not a flag.

148
00:12:53,401 --> 00:12:55,146
Governance sits above all of it.

149
00:12:55,146 --> 00:13:01,674
There is one killswitch that stops every autonomous writer,
and every automated path checks it before doing anything.

150
00:13:01,674 --> 00:13:10,734
Approval thresholds are set per action here, so a team can let the system merge duplicate tracks on its own while still
requiring a human for an ontology change.

151
00:13:10,734 --> 00:13:15,618
Every long running piece of work is a job with a state, a
progress figure and a log.

152
00:13:15,618 --> 00:13:22,267
Jobs that die are reaped rather than left claiming to run,
which sounds obvious and took a real bug to get right.

153
00:13:22,267 --> 00:13:26,823
One chronological record of everything that changed, by
machine or by person.

154
00:13:26,823 --> 00:13:30,600
This is what you read when a number moved and nobody knows
why.

155
00:13:30,600 --> 00:13:34,399
The reasoner explains a decision in words, with the evidence
attached.

156
00:13:34,399 --> 00:13:39,079
Why this class was blocked, why this lot was rejected, why
this model did not promote.

157
00:13:39,079 --> 00:13:46,733
The words are generated from the same fields the gate read, so an explanation cannot flatter a decision that the evidence
does not support.

158
00:13:46,733 --> 00:13:50,114
The data engine is the loop that decides what to label next.

159
00:13:50,114 --> 00:13:51,718
More data is not better data.

160
00:13:51,718 --> 00:13:56,933
The question is always which thousand frames would move the
number that is currently stuck.

161
00:13:56,933 --> 00:14:03,733
Frames are embedded as vectors, 42,088 of them, so you can
search the corpus by resemblance rather than by keyword.

162
00:14:03,733 --> 00:14:08,266
Show me more scenes like this one is a query the system can
answer directly.

163
00:14:08,266 --> 00:14:20,999
Text search runs over classes, attributes, sessions and scenario descriptions, and combines with the vector search, so a natural request narrows to a set of frames you can send
straight to a labelling job.

164
00:14:20,999 --> 00:14:24,255
Discovery looks for what the ontology has no word for.

165
00:14:24,255 --> 00:14:29,722
Clusters of frames that no class explains well are surfaced as
candidates for a new class.

166
00:14:29,722 --> 00:14:35,066
This is how an Indian road corpus grows a vocabulary that a
western ontology never had.

167
00:14:35,066 --> 00:14:35,932
Sievyx filters.

168
00:14:35,932 --> 00:14:41,688
Near duplicate frames, frames that are almost all sky, frames
the camera auto exposure ruined.

169
00:14:41,688 --> 00:14:50,599
Removing these before labelling is the cheapest quality improvement available, because a duplicate frame costs a full
label and teaches nothing.

170
00:14:50,599 --> 00:14:52,983
The long tail is the whole problem in India.

171
00:14:52,983 --> 00:14:58,915
Cattle on the carriageway, three people on one motorcycle, an
auto rickshaw reversing, a hand cart at night.

172
00:14:58,915 --> 00:15:06,732
These classes are rare in any corpus and over represented in the situations that matter, and this page ranks them by how
starved each one is.

173
00:15:06,732 --> 00:15:07,883
This closes the loop.

174
00:15:07,883 --> 00:15:17,785
A blocked gate produces a recall deficit per class, the deficit builds its own review batch, the batch produces
labels, the labels retrain the model and the gate runs again.

175
00:15:17,785 --> 00:15:23,599
That loop cleared a class that had been stuck at zero point
one four recall for five training cycles.

176
00:15:23,599 --> 00:15:26,987
A campaign is a labelling goal with a budget and a deadline.

177
00:15:26,987 --> 00:15:35,599
The system allocates it against the marginal value of a label per rupee, using the measured time a verdict actually takes
rather than an assumed rate.

178
00:15:35,599 --> 00:15:43,405
Interesting moments are exported as OpenSCENARIO files, so a situation found in real footage becomes a test case a
simulator can replay.

179
00:15:43,405 --> 00:15:51,732
Where the esmini simulator is installed, each exported scenario is replayed at export time and the bundle records
whether it was actually valid.

180
00:15:51,732 --> 00:15:53,291
Corpus level analytics.

181
00:15:53,291 --> 00:15:59,951
Class balance, geographic coverage, time of day, weather, how
much of each session is labelled.

182
00:15:59,951 --> 00:16:04,345
One number here is uncomfortable and deliberately left
visible.

183
00:16:04,345 --> 00:16:07,533
Of 598,179 objects, 788 carry a human verdict.

184
00:16:07,533 --> 00:16:16,532
The machine has run far ahead of the people, and pretending otherwise would make every quality number on this tour
meaningless.

185
00:16:16,532 --> 00:16:19,571
A dataset is built from a query rather than a folder.

186
00:16:19,571 --> 00:16:25,940
All night time frames containing a rider without a helmet,
excluding the gold set, excluding synthetic frames.

187
00:16:25,940 --> 00:16:30,732
The result is frozen with a hash and a datasheet that states
exactly what went in.

188
00:16:30,732 --> 00:16:36,218
Synthetic frames exist, built by pasting real instances of
starved classes into real backgrounds.

189
00:16:36,218 --> 00:16:39,475
They are quarantined structurally rather than by a filter.

190
00:16:39,475 --> 00:16:47,589
A synthetic label carries its own state and its own source, so every reader that measures anything excludes them without
having to remember to.

191
00:16:47,589 --> 00:16:51,132
No synthetic pixel can reach a validation split or a gold set.

192
00:16:51,132 --> 00:16:56,886
Indian data protection law applies to road footage, because
faces and number plates are personal data.

193
00:16:56,886 --> 00:17:06,399
Faces and plates are detected and redacted, and the redaction is verified by re-running detection on the redacted image
rather than trusting that the blur was applied.

194
00:17:06,399 --> 00:17:13,475
Access to unredacted imagery is a role, every access is logged
with a reason, and the log is not deletable from the product.

195
00:17:13,475 --> 00:17:16,639
Aggregated releases carry a differential privacy budget.

196
00:17:16,639 --> 00:17:27,799
Counts are noised, map cells with fewer than ten sessions are suppressed entirely, and when the budget for a scope is spent the endpoint refuses rather than quietly returning a weaker
guarantee.

197
00:17:27,799 --> 00:17:30,489
The same spine runs a physical security pack.

198
00:17:30,489 --> 00:17:32,017
Cameras, zones, incidents.

199
00:17:32,017 --> 00:17:38,558
It shares ingest, annotation, review, measurement and
governance, and shares none of the road specific code.

200
00:17:38,558 --> 00:17:43,999
An import contract is enforced in the build so that the core
can never reach into a pack.

201
00:17:43,999 --> 00:17:47,709
Incidents in the security pack are assembled from tracks and
zones.

202
00:17:47,709 --> 00:17:50,351
There is no transcription anywhere in this pack.

203
00:17:50,351 --> 00:17:57,266
No text is read off a frame and no text content is stored, which is a deliberate scope limit rather than a missing
feature.

204
00:17:57,266 --> 00:18:00,386
Exports go out in the formats teams already use.

205
00:18:00,386 --> 00:18:04,569
COCO, YOLO, KITTI, nuScenes, OpenLABEL and the scenario
formats.

206
00:18:04,569 --> 00:18:13,066
Every bundle carries a datasheet with the query that built it, the class counts, the synthetic share and the privacy
parameters.

207
00:18:13,066 --> 00:18:15,485
Forgyx takes a trained model to the edge.

208
00:18:15,485 --> 00:18:19,718
Export, quantise to eight bit, compile for the target and
benchmark it.

209
00:18:19,718 --> 00:18:26,733
A model that is accurate and too slow has not solved the
problem, so latency is a gate condition and not a footnote.

210
00:18:26,733 --> 00:18:27,813
Deployment is staged.

211
00:18:27,813 --> 00:18:33,000
A candidate runs in shadow beside the current model on the
vehicle before it takes over anything.

212
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:42,400
The distillation loop here trains a small student from a large teacher and repeats until it fits the latency budget, or stops
after four rounds and reports that it could not.

213
00:18:42,400 --> 00:18:43,959
Deployed models report back.

214
00:18:43,959 --> 00:18:48,116
Latency, thermal state, frames dropped and where the model was
uncertain.

215
00:18:48,116 --> 00:18:54,467
Uncertain frames come home and re-enter the labelling queue,
which is the outer loop of the whole data engine.

216
00:18:54,467 --> 00:18:56,166
Sanyx watches the hardware.

217
00:18:56,166 --> 00:19:01,134
Device temperature, storage, camera health and clock drift
across the fleet.

218
00:19:01,134 --> 00:19:03,002
Predicted failures ahead of time.

219
00:19:03,002 --> 00:19:06,562
A camera whose focus is slowly drifting or a drive filling up.

220
00:19:06,562 --> 00:19:14,734
A vehicle that records unusable footage for a week is more expensive than one that stops, because nobody notices until
the labelling starts.

221
00:19:14,734 --> 00:19:17,168
Operations shows the system's own health.

222
00:19:17,168 --> 00:19:20,636
Queue depths, GPU memory, worker state and the killswitch.

223
00:19:20,636 --> 00:19:25,139
The resource rules here are the reason this whole tour runs on
one desktop.

224
00:19:25,139 --> 00:19:31,467
Every heavy path takes a slot, works in batches, checks free
memory between them and yields to training.

225
00:19:31,467 --> 00:19:37,938
Several people work the same corpus at once, with assignment,
locking and comment threads on individual objects.

226
00:19:37,938 --> 00:19:45,867
The interface is translated into Hindi, Kannada and Tamil, because the people doing the labelling are frequently not
working in English.

227
00:19:45,867 --> 00:19:51,317
Each person sees their own throughput, their agreement with
control samples and their history.

228
00:19:51,317 --> 00:19:55,067
Measured, not estimated, and visible to the person it
describes.

229
00:19:55,067 --> 00:19:56,603
That is the full system.

230
00:19:56,603 --> 00:20:07,425
What is real today is the ingest, the auto labelling, the review surfaces, the tracking, the measurement with honest
intervals, the governance and the export path.

231
00:20:07,425 --> 00:20:19,382
What is thin today is human verdicts, with 788 of 598,179 objects ruled on, and measured ego pose, where the 154 poses
from this KITTI import are the first the corpus has ever had.

232
00:20:19,382 --> 00:20:27,332
Both of those are visible on the pages you just saw, because a
data engine that hides its own gaps is not a data engine.

233
00:20:27,332 --> 00:20:28,200
It is a demo.
